   "id": "spcpt_hT4c2P6W7Gk6NrsS",
   "name": "Gravitational potential energy",
   "definition": null,
   "video_id": "832513067"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Gravitational potential energy"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Extended Tier Only"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Energy in an object's gravitational potential energy store is defined as: **The energy an object has due to its height in a gravitational field**\n- **Work **is done against the **weight **force exerted on the object; therefore, energy is transferred\n- This means that:\n\n  - if an object is **lifted **up, energy will be transferred **to** its gravitational potential store\n  - if an object is **lowered**, energy will be transferred **away from** its gravitational potential store"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Gravitational potential energy of an object lifted through a gravitational field"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2022/08/1-7-3-gravitational-potential-energy-cie-igcse-23-rn.png",
   "file": "assets/a873674681ac-1-7-3-gravitational-potential-energy-cie-igcse-2.png",
   "alt": "1-7-3-gravitational-potential-energy-cie-igcse-23-rn",
   "caption": null
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "***Energy is transferred to the mass's gravitational store as it is lifted through the gravitational field***"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "Gravitational potential energy equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The change in energy in an object's gravitational potential energy store can be calculated using the equation:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\DeltaE_{p}=mg\\Deltah$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where:\n\n  - $\\DeltaE_{p}$ = change in gravitational potential energy, measured in joules (J)\n  - $m$ = mass, measured in kilograms (kg)\n  - $g$ = gravitational field strength, measured in newtons per kilogram (N/kg)\n  - $\\Deltah$ = change in height, measured in metres (m)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "A man climbs a flight of stairs that is, in total, 3.0 m higher than the floor. The man has a mass of 72 kg, and the gravitational field strength on Earth is approximately 9.8 N/kg.\n\nCalculate the energy transferred to the man's gravitational potential energy store.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n**Step 1: List the known quantities**\n\n- Mass of the man, $m=72\\mathrm{kg}$\n- Gravitational field strength, $g=9.8N/\\mathrm{kg}$\n- Change in height, $\\Deltah=3.0m$\n\n**Step 2: Write down the equation for gravitational potential energy**\n\n$\\DeltaE_{p}=mg\\Deltah$\n\n**Step 3: Calculate the gravitational potential energy**\n\n$\\DeltaE_{p}=72\\times9.8\\times3.0$\n\n$\\DeltaE_{p}=2116.8J$\n\n**Step 4: Round to an appropriate number of significant figures**\n\n- The input values are to 2 s.f. therefore, the output value must be to 2 s.f.\n\n`increment E_ p=2100 straight J (2 straight s. straight f.)`"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "In your exam, you will generally be expected to round your answers to an appropriate number of significant figures.\n\nYour answer can only be as accurate as your least accurate input value. Therefore, your final answer should round to the lowest number of significant figures of all your input values."
  }
 ]
}